From 9b0bc4a551d1903b24471f18e7e0b5e6e362289a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justin Gasper <jmgasper@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:23:55 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] zink/hgl: present at any desktop scale, and a cheaper bitmap
 path (haiku#109)

On a desktop drawn at 200 % a direct window was never connected: app_server
leaves a direct window alone at a scale other than 100 % unless it asked for
B_DIRECT_DEVICE_PIXELS, and nothing asked. Every frame then went through the
window system's bitmap, which app_server enlarged on the processor: about
5.3 ms of app_server per 1600x900 frame, ~95 fps for a program that was
nearly idle.

- The renderer asks for B_DIRECT_DEVICE_PIXELS on a BDirectWindow when it is
  attached (HGL_NO_DEVICE_PIXELS=1 opts out). The window system then speaks in
  frame buffer pixels. BGLView cuts its announcement down to the view with
  frame buffer and view pixels mixed up, so the renderer reads the original
  (BGLView::fClipInfo, reached through an explicit instantiation) and places
  the view itself, rounding as DirectWindowInfo does.
- zink enlarges the frame on the GPU into an image of the size the view
  covers (nearest for whole scales, linear otherwise), and copies the visible
  rectangles into the frame buffer from there. The enlarged image is kept for
  putting the last frame back.
- The window system is told that its copy of those rectangles is out of date
  (BDirectWindow::MarkDrawingBitsStale), at most once a display frame or when
  the visible parts change, never waiting for the window lock, so moves and
  screen reads take the pixels from the frame buffer.
- Putting the last frame back when an idle window is uncovered never
  happened: the wait after a move refused it, and a success would have kept
  the placement lock. It now skips that wait, releases the lock, and runs
  once more 50 ms later from a helper thread, after app_server's own repaint
  of the exposed area has reached the screen.

Bitmap path (ordinary windows):
- Each display target has a page-aligned area of its own (BBitmap on a client
  area). app_server's allocator packed a window's two bitmaps back to back, so
  unless a frame was a whole number of pages every other one failed the host
  import's alignment and was read back and copied by the processor.
- Display targets are destroyed with their resources (they leaked before),
  after letting go of the GPU's import of their memory, and freed two resizes
  later in case a window thread is still drawing the old one.
- DrawBitmapAsync + Flush, waiting only before handing over the next bitmap,
  so app_server draws one frame while the program renders the next.
- The screen and colour space are asked once a second instead of in every
  LockGL and SwapBuffers.

X399, GTX 1070, 3840x2160 at 200 %, gltest mode 2:
  direct 1600x900   95 -> 520 fps, drawing thread 1.25 -> 0.44 ms/frame,
                    app_server 5.3 ms -> ~0.03 ms per frame
  direct 800x600   260 -> (not limited by presenting)
  ordinary 1600x900 98 -> 134, 800x600 271 -> 382, 1920x1080 76 -> 119 fps
GLTeapot (view under a menu bar) at the right place; move, resize, hide and
idle-uncover runs clean; no app_server area growth over a resize soak.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
